    "details": {
      "alignment": "Neutral",
      "type": {
        "value": "humanoid",
        "subtype": "Rakasha"
      },
      "cr": 3,
      "biography": {
        "value": "<p><em>Waluipedia cast sheet — Chief Thornpaw.</em> Built by <code>tools/build-character-sheets.py</code>, hand-authored from the article. XP ledger: level 4, power rating 7 → CR 3 as a officer. Numbers are a GM-ready reading of the record, not a filed stat line; the article stays the authority. Archive date 1040 BF.</p><p><strong>The Rakasha Who Loved a Princess</strong> — Rakasha Tribes (Chief)</p><p>Status: Active — still leading his people after eighty-five years, which raises questions about Rakasha lifespans</p><p>Chief Thornpaw is the leader of the Rakasha and—according to his own testimony at Fawful's gala—was involved in a secret relationship with Princess Peach before her assassination. He claims to have been shown the secret hatch in her chambers, to have visited her privately through that passage, and to have fathered a child with her. If his testimony is accurate, he is one of the most important witnesses to the events surrounding the assassination. If his testimony is fabricated or manipulated, he may be something else entirely—a plant, a patsy, or a participant in whatever actually happened. Waluigi has learned to be careful about accepting testimony at face value, even testimony that seems emotionally authentic. Especially testimony that seems emotionally authentic. Grief can be performed. Love can be invented. And Thornpaw has had eighty-five years to construct whatever narrative serves his purposes. WAH.</p><h3>Evidence</h3><ul><li><strong>Claws</strong> — “The Rakasha Who Loved a Princess”</li><li><strong>Hunting Spear</strong> — “The Rakasha Who Loved a Princess”</li><li><strong>Multiattack</strong> — “The Rakasha Who Loved a Princess”</li><li><strong>Chief by Wisdom and Strength</strong> — “Rakasha society is organized around tribal structures led by chiefs who earn their positions through demonstrated wisdom and strength rather than hereditary succession.”</li><li><strong>Eighty-Five Years of Narrative</strong> — “And Thornpaw has had eighty-five years to construct whatever narrative serves his purposes.”</li></ul><p>Article: <code>#/article/chief_thornpaw</code> (characters). Site sheet: <code>#/sheets/chief_thornpaw</code>.</p>",
        "public": ""
      }
    },
